# How many independent offsets each fusion's composed figure cannot see. One row per fusion that
# has at least one resolved part: how many parts it has, and how many independent offsets between
# them leave the composed figure where it is (`kernel_dim`).
#
# What the number is
#   A part belongs to exactly one fusion, and a fusion adds up its parts after converting each by
#   its own factor. So each fusion with a part reads one figure, and what is left of its parts is
#   `parts - 1` independent offsets the figure cannot see. Summed over the corpus, it is every
#   offset the composition hides.
#
# A count of independent offsets, never of the offsets themselves
#   The offsets a composer could actually make are endless. Two parts allow one independent offset,
#   and infinitely many transfers along it. Reading this column as "how many ways supply can move"
#   is the same error as reading `cycle_space_dim` as the number of loops a walker could take.
#
# What a row says a fusion declared
#   `pm:Layer` defines a layer as a place where a remainder can be held independently of every other
#   layer, and `asrt:Fusion` turns that into: fuse only what is fungible. A fusion with two parts
#   adds their converted figures, so moving one unit of composed supply from one part to the other
#   leaves the composed figure exactly where it was. That is not a side effect of the arithmetic; it
#   is the claim the fusion makes, and this relation is how much of it there is.
#   `asrt:Fusion/observed` is where the composer argues for it in prose, and
#   `queries/observations/22-declared-offsets.sqlc` puts the two beside each other.
#
# The count is exact; the direction carries the factors' range
#   An offset is one unit of composed supply at one part against one unit at another, which in the
#   parts' own units is one over each part's factor. How many there are is decided by which parts
#   belong to which fusion alone, and is a whole number; which way each one points is decided by the
#   factors, and carries their range. So this relation is exact, and anything built on the
#   directions is known only as closely as the factors are.
#
# Resolved parts, because only those have figures
#   `folds/fusion_parts.sqlc` counts over `composition/part_references.sqlc` instead: what the
#   composer filed, whether or not this corpus holds the filing it names. A part that does not
#   resolve has no figures, so it adds nothing here. The two relations agree exactly where nothing
#   is unresolved, and `composition/unresolved_parts.sqlc` is the set where they would part. Read
#   that relation before concluding the two counts are interchangeable.
#
# A one-part fusion hides nothing
#   With one part there is nothing to offset against, and `kernel_dim` is zero, which is why a
#   one-part fusion asserts no fungibility at all. `checks/one_part_fusion_alters_its_part.sqlc` is
#   the rule that holds its figures to its part's, and it is scoped to exactly that case.
#
# A fusion with no resolved part has no row
#   As in `folds/fusion_parts.sqlc`, and for one more reason: a fusion with no parts would count
#   `-1`. `algebra/fusions_have_parts.sqlc` holds that no declared fusion is in that state, so this
#   relation depends on that law rather than asking its question again.
#
# Probe
#   `algebra/composition_kernel.sqlc` holds the sum against the layer graph's own node and component
#   counts. Add a part to a two-part fusion and the sum must rise by one while the layer graph's
#   node count rises by one too; a change that moves one and not the other is reading the wrong
#   count.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part counted per fusion: its parts, and the offsets that count fixes.
SELECT p.composition,
       p.composed_layer,
       count(*)     AS parts,
       count(*) - 1 AS kernel_dim
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
GROUP BY p.composition, p.composed_layer
